00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.309 align:start position:0%
 
it's great to welcome to the program  today Sheri Berman who's a professor of  political science at Barnard College at  Columbia University most recent book is  democracy and dictatorship in Europe  from the and I hope I'm pronouncing it  correctly the ancien régime to the  present days is that how it said that's  perfect good ok all right so let's so  let's jump right into it we've been  talking a lot about social democracy in  the context of what are the differences  between that and democratic socialism  how does this compare to different types  of capitalism and one of the interesting  areas of this is that much of sort of  modern social democracy socially  democratic thoughts stems from early  20th century Sweden and the Social  Democratic Party and if we look today at  Europe and what's going on that model  has actually changed significantly with  the rise in right-wing nationalism in  Europe and a lot of other changes that  are taking place so how would you if  someone just says to you you know sherry  how has the state of social democracy  changed over the last hundred years in  Europe how would you generally  characterize it well so there's lots of  different ways to answer that question  which is never a good answer to a  question I mean as far as the sort of  status of parties that call themselves  social democratic or would be seen as  kind of center-left we have seen a  dramatic decline in support for these  parties over the late 20th and early  21st century so one way to tell this  story is to talk about the rise of these  parties elect early politically during  the early 20th century they're sort of  greatest electoral success after the  Second World War and then a decline that  again has set in over the last decades  on the other way to tell the story is by  focusing less on how many votes they get  and more on their ideas and how powerful  those ideas have been the traditionally  are the sort of distinctive social  democratic view is one that accepts  capitalism but is wary of it and  believes that it is necessary for the  democratic state to intervene  in markets and in the economic sphere in  general to sort of protect citizens from  the most destructive and destabilizing  consequences of markets and again if  we're gonna look at it that way then one  could say starting in the 20th the  beginning of the 20th century Social  Democrats within the left started  fighting for this view against folks  further to the lapped Marxists  communists Democratic socialists only in  Scandinavia and particularly in Sweden  were these kinds of social democratic  views able to triumph on the left during  the interwar period but after the Second  World War these type of views come to  dominate mainstream center-left and  Social Democratic parties as well as by  the way most other parties in Europe on  the centre and center right so this  basic social democratic view that it was  the task of democratic states to control  capitalism to tour rather to tame  capitalism to protect citizens this was  basically accepted across the board in  Europe after the Second World War but by  the end of the 20th century this  consensus begins to fray and in fact  becomes largely replaced by a more  neoliberal consensus which reigns you  know sort of up through I would say the  financial and euro crisis and now we're  back again to an era when that consensus  that more neoliberal consensus is being  questioned and we're again debating some  fundamental questions about the  relationship between democracy and  capitalism the correct role and scope  for markets things of that nature  is there a phrase in a different way are  there places in Europe where as as a  sort of idea labeled as social democracy  many of those ideas are just sort of  mainstream and as such it's not as  important that they be labeled in that  way so when you look at for example the  healthcare systems of many European  countries the debate over well it's the  Social Democrats who want one type of  system and they're pushing against  everybody else who wants a sort of more  American  type system aren't a lot of those  institutions now just kind of part of  the baseline and as such they're no  longer labeled as socially democratic so  I think that's I mean that is absolutely  correct unlike in the United States  citizens in Europe since 1945 have come  to take for granted a whole range of  social protections that we are still  fighting over here in the United States  and you mentioned the most obvious ones  universal health care pre education a  whole variety of other social  protections that we do not have as a  matter of citizenship here in the u.s.  in that sense a sort of social  democratic view of the relationship  between states and markets still reigns  but these kinds of systems have come  under stress in Europe and they have  been cheap and they have changed fairly  dramatically over the past decade so  while you still have a very extensive  welfare state for example in Europe and  while in places like Sweden you know you  still have high levels of government  spending everybody expects health care  pensions education etc to be given as a  matter of course you have seen  significant privatization of many of  these policies you've seen rising  inequality and you've seen some  deterioration around the edge in the  kinds of commitments States have made to  citizens so yes um no in Europe it is  generally accepted that you know welfare  states are a good thing and that  governments do need to provide social  protection for citizens but there has  been an erosion of many of these  policies and more importantly perhaps  many of the principles upon which they  were predicated after the Second World  War so in any election there is always  what we would call some level of  defection people who in a primary vote  on one side and end up voting for in the  general election for someone from a  different party there's defection of all  kinds but we have some data specifically  that 12% of Boerne primary voters in  2016 ended up voting for Donald Trump  now they were you can argue balanced out  by some you know Ted Cruz voters who  ended up voting for Hillary Clinton but  to focus in specifically for the  purposes of our conversation on those  12% is that a phenomenon that is also  identifiable in Europe where European  Social Democrats and folks more aligned  with the ideology of Bernie Sanders gets  sort of swept up in some of these  populist right-wing movements are in the  nationalism and end up supporting that  or is that not a dynamic you've observed  in Europe so the answer to your question  again is yes and no okay I mean look at  any American election the candidate in  the primaries who what the supporters of  primary candidates who lose some  percentage of them always defect to the  other party so the 12% of Bernie Sanders  voters who defected to Trump is not out  of line with the number of Clinton  supporters who defected after Clinton  lost to Obama but the larger point that  you raise is a very important one the  defection of lower educated and  working-class white voters from the  Democratic Party to the Republican Party  and particularly to Trump in 2016 this  is a broader phenomenon that we have  seen in Europe in fact in many European  countries now it is not social  democratic parties but right-wing  populist parties that are the largest  working-class parties in their party  systems that is to say more workers  people in the working-class support  right-wing populist parties in many  European countries including France and  Austria than they do social democratic  parties so the broader phenomenon right  folks defecting from the left  particularly again among the  working-class and people with lower  educational levels to the populist right  this is a profoundly important and  destabilizing phenomenon that you can  see on both sides of the Atlantic in the  book sort of related to this one of the  things you talk about our countries in  Europe that have formed I guess what we  would call liberal Western democracies  in the last hundred several hundred  years and that at different points in  time and for different reasons there's  sort of a backsliding that happens  towards autocracy towards  authoritarianism what are the more  common reasons if there are unifying or  common reasons that that happen  there are they really unique cases well  there's lots of reasons why they happen  and I think it is important given how  worried we are about the state of  democracy today to point out that we  still have more democracies in the world  today than ever before and that we've  had less backsliding after the wave of  democratization that began in the late  20th century with the collapse of the  Soviet Union then we have during  previous democratic waves so those would  be waves in 1848 after the first world  war after the Second World War  so overall the state of democracy is  still quite strong but for countries  that do backslide in places where  democracy does decay if not collapse  there are definitely some patterns that  you know we should be wary of because in  the past they have led to again  significant problems with democracy  social polarization which of course is  something we are constantly talking  about here in the US this is very  problematic because it makes democracy  almost impossible to function people see  their opponents as illegitimate rather  than just as opponents they find it very  difficult to compromise in bargain which  of course is what democracy requires  every political battle becomes zero-sum  and so it's very difficult to have  well-functioning democracies in the  kinds of situations where you have  social polarization parties are pushed  further to the extreme that kind of  thing in general if we think about the  sort of period when democracy  quote-unquote work best in the West that  is to say after the Second World War  when we got stable well-functioning  democracies in Western Europe as well as  obviously well-functioning democracy  here in the United States we should also  remember that this was a period with  very particular social and economic  characteristics as well we had rising  growth Europe grew the fastest it's ever  grown in its entire history in the 30  years after 1945 and along with that  growth came incredible equalization of  incomes and wealth again greater  equalization that had ever occurred  before and this is important because it  helped to diminish divisions in society  it helped to create the sense that the  democratic system could deliver for  everyone that the gains of one group did  not  come at the expense of the others and  that politicians and parties were  responsive to the needs of their  citizens of course we all know that this  kind of these kinds of developments  change dramatically in the late 20th  century when growth slowed unemployment  rose particularly in Europe inequality  skyrocketed in the United States in  particular but in almost all European  countries as well and we began to see  again greater divisions within Western  societies all of which were accompanied  by dramatic social and demographic  change which only complicated the  process of democratic politics even more  absolutely fascinating stuff and I think  anybody who has listened to our  conversation can tell that there is a  lot to be gleaned from the history of  Europe in terms of what we are starting  to see in some ways in the United States  today the book is democracy and  dictatorship in Europe from the ancien  régime to the present day we've been  speaking with the book's author Barnard  College professor sherry Berman sherry  really a pleasure having you on thank  you so much I was really glad to have  this conversation

